# Stage 1: example B1 with a kaon+ beam, everything P9-P13 owes inactivated,
# and Decay LEFT ACTIVE.
#
# The kaon is the shortest-lived of the three positives - 12.380 ns proper, against the
# pion's 26.033 - and the slowest at 200 MeV (beta 0.703), so it decays the most: about
# 10% of tracks inside B1. Its range is 411.1 mm against the 300 mm envelope, so it still
# crosses. The largest decay signal of the nine rows.
#
# THE _noelastic VARIANT. `hadElastic` is inactivated here as well, so this is the
# like-for-like for a port that has DECAY AND NOT ELASTIC. The difference between this
# file's dose and stage1_kaon_plus.mac's is what hadElastic is worth for this species and
# this geometry - a measurement of the remaining gap rather than a claim about it.
# See stage1_README.md.
#
# Every name below is a row of ref/oracle/species_processes.csv for kaon+ - the dump of
# the constructed QBBC's own process manager. /process/inactivate ignores a name the
# species does not have, silently, so a macro written from the source rather than from
# the object can look like it switched something off. docs/RISK.md V43.

/run/initialize
/control/verbose 0
/run/verbose 1

/process/inactivate hBrems
/process/inactivate hPairProd
/process/inactivate kaon+Inelastic
/process/inactivate hadElastic

# The stage, recorded by what RAN and not by what was intended. docs/RISK.md V43's
# lesson: ask the object. This prints Active/InActive for every process on the
# particle's manager, after the inactivations above.
/particle/select kaon+
/particle/process/dump

/gun/particle kaon+
/gun/energy 200 MeV

/run/printProgress 100000
/run/beamOn 500000
